WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.030 --> 00:00:05.039
Hello everyone, welcome to the Langfocus Channel and my name is Paul. Today

00:00:05.040 --> 00:00:09.960
we're going to go back in time and talk about an important influence on the English

00:00:10.120 --> 00:00:15.360
language, the influence of the Vikings and their language Old Norse. It's almost

00:00:15.879 --> 00:00:22.948
universally accepted that English is a West Germanic language along with Frisian, Dutch and German and that modern English

00:00:23.140 --> 00:00:24.820
descended from Anglo-Saxon

00:00:24.820 --> 00:00:29.099
also known as Old English the language spoken by Germanic tribes that

00:00:29.230 --> 00:00:35.160
migrated to Great Britain in the fifth century. As I mentioned in my video: is English really a Germanic language?

00:00:35.739 --> 00:00:42.569
English vocabulary was highly influenced by French after the Norman invasion in the 11th century in the subsequent

00:00:42.760 --> 00:00:44.760
control of England by the Anglo-Normans.

00:00:45.309 --> 00:00:47.309
But there's one more piece of the puzzle

00:00:48.489 --> 00:00:54.119
English was also highly influenced by Old Norse after Viking invasions and Danish and Norwegian

00:00:54.730 --> 00:00:57.389
settlements in the 8th, 9th and 10th centuries.

00:00:58.809 --> 00:01:04.319
A large section of England was under Danish Viking control from the year 878 to

00:01:04.720 --> 00:01:09.479
954 at its largest around half of England under a regime known as the Danelaw.

00:01:10.180 --> 00:01:17.639
Both English and Scandinavians lived in the area of the Danelaw and through extensive interaction and intermarriage the culture of the area

00:01:17.890 --> 00:01:19.299
became heavily

00:01:19.299 --> 00:01:25.199
Scandinavian influenced and of course there was contact between their languages: Old English and Old Norse.

00:01:26.140 --> 00:01:31.619
After the English reasserted control over this area, the culture of the area remained heavily

00:01:31.900 --> 00:01:35.639
Scandinavian influenced and Danish attacks against England continued.

00:01:36.159 --> 00:01:43.139
There was even a period of 26 years from 1016 to 1042 when the Danes controlled all of England.

00:01:44.200 --> 00:01:49.470
This period of Viking influence continued until the Norman invasions which began in 1066.

00:01:50.079 --> 00:01:57.659
The exact linguistic situation of that time period is not known but we know that Old English and Old Norse were closely related.

00:01:57.939 --> 00:02:00.419
Before the Viking invasions began in the year

00:02:00.850 --> 00:02:06.600
793, the Anglo-Saxon tribes had arrived from across the sea between the 5th and 7th centuries.

00:02:07.329 --> 00:02:12.299
They spoke Ingvaeonic dialect, Anglo-Frisian and Old Saxon dialect, and

00:02:12.670 --> 00:02:15.870
existed in close proximity with speakers of Proto-Norse.

00:02:16.330 --> 00:02:20.999
Before the Anglo-Saxon migration, these dialects were likely all mutually intelligible,

00:02:22.000 --> 00:02:24.870
so with only a couple of hundred years between the

00:02:25.150 --> 00:02:30.179
Anglo-Saxon migration and the Viking invasions their languages were likely still mutually intelligible

00:02:30.280 --> 00:02:35.430
at least to a limited extent, at the very least there were many similar cognate words.

00:02:35.950 --> 00:02:43.349
Despite the Germanic connection between Old English and Old Norse we can see that the differences between Old English and Middle English are striking.

00:02:43.570 --> 00:02:50.969
That English underwent a change and simplification in grammar and that many Old Norse words were adopted into English.

00:02:51.100 --> 00:02:58.230
Vocabulary. As we know, Norman French had a big impact on Middle English making up around 30% of English vocabulary

00:02:58.540 --> 00:03:04.950
by the end of the Middle English period, but if we leave French aside and just look at the Germanic vocabulary of English

00:03:04.950 --> 00:03:11.129
it looks like this: words with cognates in both Old English and Old Norse make up 50% of the words,

00:03:11.230 --> 00:03:18.329
words found only in Old English make up 36%, and words found only in Old Norse make up 14%.

00:03:18.790 --> 00:03:23.010
You might be thinking only 14%? That's not so many Old Norse words!

00:03:23.020 --> 00:03:27.329
But also notice that 50% of the words had cognates in both languages.

00:03:27.330 --> 00:03:30.719
This could mean that as Middle English developed, the English and

00:03:31.090 --> 00:03:38.700
Scandinavians had the tendency to choose words that were common to both languages as this would obviously make communication easier and while

00:03:39.160 --> 00:03:41.639
14% may seem like a fairly low number

00:03:41.640 --> 00:03:47.070
a lot of those words are very basic and common words that's important because when a language borrows

00:03:47.290 --> 00:03:48.540
vocabulary from another one

00:03:48.540 --> 00:03:55.290
it usually borrows words that are more advanced or more literary or words for concepts that couldn't be expressed in the language before.

00:03:55.450 --> 00:04:02.309
Borrowing basic words usually requires an intense prolonged period of contact between the two languages.

00:04:03.010 --> 00:04:09.839
Here are some examples of basic words that entered English from Old Norse. The third-person plural pronouns came from Old Norse.

00:04:10.090 --> 00:04:17.910
These are the ancestors of "they", "them" and "their"; words related to animals "reindeer" from Old Norse "hreindyr";

00:04:18.640 --> 00:04:26.609
"gosling" from Old Norse "gaeslingr" which was borrowed into Middle English as "gesling"; the word for "egg" from Old Norse "egg";

00:04:27.240 --> 00:04:29.490
"wing" from Old Norse "vaengr",

00:04:29.490 --> 00:04:32.400
which became Middle English "winge" or "wenge".

00:04:32.889 --> 00:04:36.598
Nature and landscape: "bark" from Old Norse "borkr";

00:04:36.819 --> 00:04:41.999
"root" from Old Norse "rót", this was borrowed into Late Old English "rot";

00:04:42.610 --> 00:04:48.300
"sky" from Old Norse "sky" and the original meaning was "cloud". Negative words:

00:04:48.759 --> 00:04:53.279
"anger" from Old Norse "angr"; "die" from Old Norse "deyja";

00:04:53.550 --> 00:05:00.479
which entered Old English as "digan", which became Middle English "deyen"; "ill" from Old Norse "illr",

00:05:00.490 --> 00:05:02.699
which entered Middle English as "ille";

00:05:03.250 --> 00:05:09.690
"rotten" from Old Norse "rotinn" which entered Middle English as "roten"; "ugly" from Old Norse

00:05:10.030 --> 00:05:14.459
"ugga" or "uggligr", which entered Middle English as "uglike";

00:05:15.280 --> 00:05:21.569
words related to the body: "leg" from Old Norse "leggr"; "skin" from Old Norse "skinn";

00:05:22.180 --> 00:05:28.799
words for common objects: "bag" from Old Norse "baggi"; "ball" from Old Norse "bollr";

00:05:29.169 --> 00:05:34.139
"knife" from Old Norse "knifr" which entered Late Old English as "cnif".

00:05:35.080 --> 00:05:41.219
Verbs: the plural present tense of the verb "to be" in other words "are" came from Old Norse "eru";

00:05:41.740 --> 00:05:49.079
"call" from Old Norse "kalla", which entered Middle English as "callen"; "hit" from Old Norse "hitta"

00:05:49.210 --> 00:05:56.609
which entered Middle English as "hitten"; "take" from Old Norse "taka" which entered Late Old English as "tacan",

00:05:56.680 --> 00:05:58.799
which became Middle English "taken".

00:05:58.900 --> 00:06:03.569
These are just some examples to give you an idea of the types of basic everyday words

00:06:03.569 --> 00:06:09.149
which were borrowed from Old Norse. Around a thousand words of Old Norse became part of standard English.

00:06:09.150 --> 00:06:15.449
But that number is significantly higher if we include some of the northern dialects of English like the Yorkshire dialect.

00:06:15.639 --> 00:06:23.639
Grammar. In the transition from Old English to Middle English, English went from being a synthetic language with lots of inflection to a highly

00:06:23.860 --> 00:06:28.289
analytic language which relies on word order and prepositions instead of inflection.

00:06:28.690 --> 00:06:36.419
Nouns lost their case inflections, nouns used to occur in four different forms: nominative, genitive, dative and accusative, and

00:06:36.880 --> 00:06:41.339
occasionally in a fifth case the instrumental case, for example in Old English

00:06:41.369 --> 00:06:47.609
there was this phrase meaning"the heavy stone" with the word for stone being a masculine strong a-stem noun:

00:06:48.219 --> 00:06:53.129
"se hefiga stan", "paes hefigan stan-es", "paem hefigan stan-e"

00:06:53.889 --> 00:07:01.859
"pone hefigan stan"; and in the plural form meaning "the heavy stones": "pa hefigan stan-as", "paera hefigra stan-a"

00:07:02.469 --> 00:07:05.849
"pa hefigum stan-um".

00:07:06.159 --> 00:07:10.528
In Middle English these different forms were mostly lost, for the singular

00:07:10.529 --> 00:07:16.229
there was just "the heviy stan" and for the genitive form, in other words the possessive, there was

00:07:16.300 --> 00:07:22.949
"the heviy stanes", in the plural there was just "the heviy stanes", with the ending of the nominative and accusative

00:07:23.229 --> 00:07:26.489
cases becoming the plural "s", in the noun

00:07:26.800 --> 00:07:33.449
distinct cases were lost except for the genitive and the different forms of the definite article were all reduced to "pe",

00:07:33.550 --> 00:07:39.269
which was a variant of "se" from Old English and unstressed it was pronounced "thə"

00:07:39.909 --> 00:07:43.349
and all case and number distinctions were lost in the adjectives.

00:07:44.050 --> 00:07:50.339
Also in Old English the definite article and adjectives were declined differently in the feminine and neuter genders.

00:07:50.339 --> 00:07:52.708
But all of those gender distinctions were lost.

00:07:53.169 --> 00:07:58.228
There were various other plural endings for different genders and classes of nouns in Old English.

00:07:58.229 --> 00:08:03.208
But the plural form from this particular class of nouns became generalized to most nouns.

00:08:04.300 --> 00:08:10.199
Another plural ending was also common "en" which still lingers on in a few words like "oxen"

00:08:10.419 --> 00:08:16.828
"brethren "and "children", though this one: "children" actually combines two plural forms "childer" plus "en".

00:08:16.899 --> 00:08:19.859
This is a form of simplification in which a particular

00:08:20.379 --> 00:08:22.379
irregular form becomes regularized.

00:08:22.990 --> 00:08:30.869
Another change, many verb inflections were also lost. Old English verbs mainly fit into two groups strong verbs and weak verbs.

00:08:31.209 --> 00:08:38.399
Strong verbs formed the past tense through a change in a vowel in the verb stem and weak verbs formed the past tense by adding

00:08:38.399 --> 00:08:44.669
a suffix after the verb stem "-ode" and "-odest" in the singular, and "-odon" in the plural.

00:08:45.130 --> 00:08:52.979
In Middle English, the past tense endings in weak class two verbs developed into regular past tense endings, which today are just "-ed".

00:08:53.540 --> 00:08:58.249
Many strong verbs disappeared and new verbs adopted the weak past tense forms.

00:08:58.320 --> 00:09:02.270
This is another example of how English became simplified and regularized.

00:09:02.760 --> 00:09:09.619
In Middle English the use of auxiliary verbs to supplement verb tenses also began. For example, in Old English

00:09:09.620 --> 00:09:11.480
there was no specific future tense.

00:09:11.480 --> 00:09:14.060
The future was expressed with the present tense conjugation.

00:09:14.550 --> 00:09:21.169
But in Middle English "schulen" meaning "shall" and "willen" meaning "will" began to be used as future markers.

00:09:21.170 --> 00:09:24.800
"he schal loke" meaning "he will look" or "he shall look" and

00:09:25.410 --> 00:09:30.410
"Silden he us wille" meaning "He will shield us", in other words "He will protect us".

00:09:30.840 --> 00:09:35.540
This was a sign of English becoming a more analytic language as it lost its inflections.

00:09:36.270 --> 00:09:44.150
The word order of English also became more fixed as English lost its inflections. In Old English "se hefiga stan" would be clearly

00:09:44.340 --> 00:09:45.030
nominative.

00:09:45.030 --> 00:09:51.589
Normally the subject of a sentence so you could move that phrase around and it would still be clear that it was the subject but

00:09:51.590 --> 00:09:55.429
In Middle English "the heviy stan" could be nominative, dative or accusative.

00:09:56.010 --> 00:09:59.090
So to show it's the subject it would normally be first

00:09:59.250 --> 00:10:03.140
followed by a verb and then an object or a compliment, if there was one.

00:10:03.720 --> 00:10:11.720
So, how did the contact between Old English and Old Norse caused or influenced these changes? A lot of this comes down to speculation, based

00:10:11.720 --> 00:10:19.099
on small bits of information that we have. Despite the violence of the initial Viking invasions, in the areas under Scandinavian rule

00:10:19.100 --> 00:10:20.820
it seems clear that the

00:10:20.820 --> 00:10:24.229
Anglo-Saxons were not driven out or kept separate from the Scandinavians.

00:10:24.540 --> 00:10:30.739
They lived in close proximity with each other and at least in certain areas and at certain times they were highly integrated.

00:10:31.470 --> 00:10:38.540
With this context in mind there are several theories about the contact between Old English and Old North. The traditionally held

00:10:38.760 --> 00:10:42.650
consensus is that Middle English descended directly from Old English without

00:10:42.930 --> 00:10:50.840
interruption and that outside influence from Old Norse did not change the West Germanic core of the language. In this traditional view Middle English

00:10:51.090 --> 00:10:53.209
developed with an Old Norse substrate

00:10:53.220 --> 00:11:00.139
this means that as Scandinavians adopted English changes entered the language through imperfect learning on their part or

00:11:00.510 --> 00:11:03.290
borrowing from Old Norse on the part of English speakers

00:11:04.170 --> 00:11:09.739
there's a minority view that Middle English was a Creole, in other words a new language that arose from

00:11:10.080 --> 00:11:13.849
contact between Old English and Old Norse with influences from both.

00:11:14.190 --> 00:11:17.749
Creoles usually arise when communities of people who share no common

00:11:17.880 --> 00:11:22.999
language come in close contact with each other and they come up with a new language out of necessity.

00:11:23.420 --> 00:11:30.740
This pidgin language is created by adults. Then, it becomes a native language of the next generation. One typical feature of Creoles

00:11:30.740 --> 00:11:36.320
is that the vocabulary is mostly derived from the higher prestige language, while its grammar is a

00:11:36.540 --> 00:11:41.480
drastically simplified version of the lower prestige languages grammar if English is a Creole

00:11:41.480 --> 00:11:45.709
that means that it cannot be classified as either West Germanic or North Germanic.

00:11:46.170 --> 00:11:53.149
But one problem with this theory is that true Creoles are usually more grammatically simplified and regularized than Middle English.

00:11:53.850 --> 00:11:58.580
Creoles usually begin as pidgin languages created by speakers of mutually unintelligible

00:11:59.279 --> 00:12:02.088
languages, so rather than combining both languages

00:12:02.089 --> 00:12:07.639
they create a simple new language from scratch and they normally don't have gender and case in pronouns,

00:12:08.070 --> 00:12:15.979
singular plural distinction verb inflections for person or tense passive forms and so on, but Middle English retained all of those things

00:12:16.410 --> 00:12:20.630
Maybe that's because Old English and Old Norse were so similar to begin with, that

00:12:20.630 --> 00:12:23.539
they didn't need to create an entirely new language to communicate.

00:12:24.000 --> 00:12:29.149
Even if some creolization did take place, if they already shared a lot of common vocabulary

00:12:29.579 --> 00:12:32.029
but the grammatical inflections caused confusion,

00:12:32.390 --> 00:12:38.809
maybe they spoke in a dumbed-down or a simplified sort of way with fewer grammatical inflections in order to communicate.

00:12:38.970 --> 00:12:42.079
That doesn't really fulfill the criteria of a pidgin

00:12:42.079 --> 00:12:48.679
but maybe we could call it a semi-pidgin or something like that, and if their children grew up in that household hearing their parents

00:12:48.680 --> 00:12:54.950
speak a semi-pidgin and they adopted that as their native language then maybe we could call that native language a semi-creole.

00:12:55.110 --> 00:13:00.380
There is also a controversial new theory that English is actually a North Germanic language.

00:13:00.380 --> 00:13:03.439
That means that Scandinavians didn't learn Old English

00:13:03.440 --> 00:13:10.489
but rather the English learned Old Norse so Middle English is actually based on Old Norse at its core with lots of

00:13:10.560 --> 00:13:12.029
influence from Old English.

00:13:12.029 --> 00:13:16.099
So according to this theory English doesn't descend directly from Old English

00:13:16.100 --> 00:13:22.850
but rather from Old Norse along with Swedish, Norwegia, Danish, Icelandic and Faroese. The basis of this theory

00:13:22.850 --> 00:13:27.589
is that the syntax of Middle English and Modern English reflects Old Norse in numerous ways.

00:13:27.779 --> 00:13:33.049
Here are some examples: in English the verbs come before the object. "I can play the piano"

00:13:33.440 --> 00:13:36.200
This is similar to the North Germanic word order in

00:13:36.450 --> 00:13:41.869
Norwegian "Jeg kan spille piano", in the West Germanic languages Dutch and German

00:13:41.870 --> 00:13:46.820
the second verb comes at the end, after the object: "Ik kan piano spelen".

00:13:47.339 --> 00:13:49.339
"Ich kann Klavier spielen"

00:13:50.070 --> 00:13:55.549
Old English was similar to Dutch and German in this regard, another difference: preposition stranding

00:13:55.920 --> 00:14:01.399
This means that prepositions can come at the end of clauses without the noun that usually follows them

00:14:01.399 --> 00:14:07.489
So in English we have "That issue was talked about" in Norwegian "Den saken ble snakket om".

00:14:08.130 --> 00:14:11.510
Preposition stranding is actually very rare among the languages of the world

00:14:12.360 --> 00:14:19.610
Split infinitives: despite whatever your high school English teacher may have taught you split infinitives are very common and normal in English.

00:14:19.829 --> 00:14:22.909
Modern North Germanic languages are the same. In English

00:14:22.910 --> 00:14:28.699
you can say "I promise to not do that again" with "to not do" in Norwegian

00:14:28.699 --> 00:14:33.559
you can say "Jeg lover a ikke gjore de igjen". In both languages

00:14:33.620 --> 00:14:36.769
the negation adverb is placed in the middle of the infinitive

00:14:36.959 --> 00:14:41.899
splitting it. Not only the negation adverb but other types of adverbs can go there as well.

00:14:42.120 --> 00:14:49.399
Split infinitives did not occur in Old English and they do not occur in Dutch or German. They did however occur occasionally in Old Norse

00:14:50.040 --> 00:14:56.120
Those are a few of 14 different syntactic elements that occur in both English and North Germanic languages

00:14:56.120 --> 00:15:02.089
but not in Old English. How do we know that these features weren't simply borrowed from Old Norse into Old English?

00:15:02.190 --> 00:15:02.690
well

00:15:02.690 --> 00:15:10.070
this theory is based on the understanding that when one language borrows from another it usually doesn't borrow structures it borrows mainly

00:15:10.380 --> 00:15:11.160
vocabulary.

00:15:11.160 --> 00:15:12.899
But I don't think that's quite true

00:15:12.899 --> 00:15:20.089
language structures are sometimes borrowed when there is extensive long term contact between the languages and when there is extensive bilingualism

00:15:20.490 --> 00:15:25.699
amongst speakers of the borrowing language. Old Norse was not simply the language of the elite,

00:15:25.699 --> 00:15:29.989
it was spoken by people living side by side with the speakers of Old English.

00:15:29.990 --> 00:15:33.620
So there was likely intense contact as well as a lot of bilingualism.

00:15:34.450 --> 00:15:39.270
Therefore, Old English certainly could have borrowed structures from Old Norse and not just vocabulary

00:15:39.270 --> 00:15:45.900
So I'm not convinced that English is based on Old Norse. In my opinion and I freely admit that I'm speculating here

00:15:45.900 --> 00:15:53.790
I think that the traditional consensus still stands, but there may also have been some degree of language mixing because of childhood bilingualism.

00:15:54.340 --> 00:16:00.509
It's quite clear that Old Norse made a bigger impact further north in the Danish controlled areas. So up there

00:16:00.510 --> 00:16:03.989
there was likely more language mixing but when Middle English was

00:16:04.150 --> 00:16:08.519
standardized, the East Midlands dialect was chosen as the basis of the standard.

00:16:08.520 --> 00:16:11.579
It was sort of the midway point between the northern more

00:16:11.860 --> 00:16:18.810
Scandinavian influenced dialects and the southern more Anglo dialects this definitely limited the amount of Norse influence,

00:16:18.810 --> 00:16:22.500
so that Modern English today is more West Germanic than North Germanic.

00:16:22.630 --> 00:16:26.549
Even if some of those northern dialects tend a little more towards North Germanic.

00:16:26.830 --> 00:16:31.799
The question of the day to speakers of North Germanic languages and people who have studied them:

00:16:31.800 --> 00:16:35.820
What kinds of similarities have you noticed between English and your language? and

00:16:35.980 --> 00:16:41.820
to speakers of other languages: Has your language been influenced by others as a result of language contact?

00:16:41.940 --> 00:16:48.119
Let's leave Modern English borrowings aside and think more about historical contact between different language communities.

00:16:48.190 --> 00:16:52.920
Be sure to follow Langfocus on Facebook, Twitter and Instagram and once again

